Yes. Once you’re completely done with a Git worktree, you can safely delete it—as long as you don’t have uncommitted work you still need.

If you’re using Git worktrees:

git worktree list

Then remove the completed worktree:

git worktree remove <path-to-worktree>

For example:

git worktree remove ../my-feature
Important

Deleting the worktree does not delete the branch. The branch remains available in your main repository.

If you also want to delete the branch after merging:

git branch -d feature/my-feature

If the branch has not been merged and you're sure you don't need it:

git branch -D feature/my-feature

A typical cleanup flow is:

git worktree list
git worktree remove ../my-feature
git branch -d feature/my-feature

So yes—after the PR is merged and you no longer need that working directory, removing the worktree is a good cleanup practice.

If you see changes that you don't need, you can remove the worktree with:

cd E:\AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\PythonProgrames
git worktree remove --force E:/AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS/python1


2. If the feature branch is already merged

Check:

git branch --merged

If you see:

feature/python1

then you can delete the branch:

git branch -d feature/python1

